<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kaninë</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Kanina</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kaninë is a village and medieval castle on a hill above <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> in south-western Albania. The fortress, with late antique origins, guarded the port of Vlorë and was held in turn by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>, the Serbian Empire, the lords of Valona and, from 1417, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>. Its walls survive as ruins.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kanine.jpg" alt="Kaninë" width="320" height="463" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Decius · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Kanina_neue_Moschee.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>The village of Kaninë grew up below the hilltop castle that overlooks it, in Vlorë County, and since the local government reform of 2015 it has been part of the municipality of Vlorë. The name was first recorded at the beginning of the 11th century in Byzantine Greek as ta Kanina, though one theory links it to a fort that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a> calls Kionin in Epirus Vetus. The linguist Bardhyl Demiraj notes that the name shows no sign of Albanian rhotacism, unlike that of nearby Vlorë, which makes it significant for the history of the Albanian language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The first fort dates to the late 4th or early 3rd century BCE and is linked to the fortified sites of the Illyrian tribes; Kaninë gave the Amantes their main route to the sea, and it has been proposed as the site of ancient Thronium. Its walls were rebuilt in late antiquity, in line with the works of Justinian in the 6th century. After near abandonment in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>, the fortress revived by the 11th century. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of Roland names it as Canelius in the fight against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/normans">Normans</a> in 1081, and as the Provincia Jericho et Caninon it appears in the chrysobull granted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/venice">Venice</a> by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexios-iii-angelos">Alexios III Angelos</a> in 1198.</p><p>In 1258 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michael-ii-komnenos-doukas">Michael II Komnenos Doukas</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/epirus">Epirus</a> gave it as dowry to his daughter Helena Angelina Doukaina on her marriage to King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manfred-king-of-sicily">Manfred of Sicily</a>. Contested between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> and the Angevins in the 1280s, it stayed with the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> until at least the 1330s. The Serbian king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> held the area from 1345 until 1372, when it passed to Balsha II. Under Ottoman rule its importance declined, the more so after the castle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vlore">Vlorë</a> was built in 1537. In the early 16th century it had 514 households, all Christian.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>In the late 17th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/evliya-celebi">Evliya Çelebi</a> described a garrison of 400 soldiers, 320 stone houses inside the walls and 300 outside, and a large mosque, a Bektashi tekke and a palace built by the landowner Sinan Pasha. None of those buildings has left a trace, but the castle still stands, visited over the years by William Leake, Johann Georg von Hahn, Carl Patsch and N.G.L. Hammond.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Kaninë” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Stefan Dušan of the Serbian Empire took Valona (Vlorë) and <b>Kanina</b> from Byzantium around 1345.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-valona">Principality of Valona</a></li></ul></section>
